#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125294} :activity/id #uuid "2f9b26dc-7c78-5bb7-9eb0-e6029679e710" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048455 :topic/id #uuid "a05758d1-f2b4-5a86-b7b0-c2a4bb6dcb85" :topic/math-academy-id 1931} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125737 :tutorial/content "We can extend our knowledge of limits to multivariable functions. We'll first consider the two-variable case.\n\nWe denote $\\mathbf{x}=(x,y)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_0 = (a,b)$ as a particular, fixed vector. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined in some neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$\n\nThen, the notation\n\n$$\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f (x,y) = L$$\n\nmeans that the value of $f$ can be made as close as we want to $L,$ provided that $\\mathbf x$ is sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{x}_0.$\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- This definition of a limit also extends to functions of more than two variables in the natural way.\n- All of the rules set out by the algebra of limits are valid for functions of several variables.\n\nWe say that $f(x,y)$ is **continuous** at $(a,b)$ if\n\n$$\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (a,b)} f (x,y) = f(a,b).$$\n\nSo, if a function is continuous at $(a,b),$ then the limit can be evaluated by simply plugging $(x,y) = (a,b)$ into the function.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the following limit: $$\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}$$\n\nThis function is continuous for all $(x,y)$ with the exception of $(0,0)$ where the denominator vanishes. Since the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(x,y) = (2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eccfdda1-288a-5169-adde-a00163fa348f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2480 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a0c271d5-ca3c-5651-a78d-a2b18242c45c" :step/math-academy-id 11087 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124262 :question/id #uuid "a5753b43-b288-5cca-a26b-b060917182ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-3330"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2ac99b-55b2-5742-81ab-d2961db1250f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380054}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions at Points of Continuity"} :step/id #uuid "9e271cad-793b-51f0-9124-47a943c42f3e" :step/math-academy-id 11090 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125335}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124263 :question/id #uuid "301263ef-3b68-54ac-87aa-5964484f5a14" :question/math-academy-id "e-3331"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5666035-199d-56aa-a6f4-f65a3f847b42" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049933}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407878}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "98229d4f-e216-5858-8b87-e9442dce8ede" :step/math-academy-id 11092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125472}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125738 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a function of a single variable $f(x),$ if the left and right limits both exist and are equal at some point $x=a$, then this is sufficient to conclude that the limit exists at $x=a.$ \n\nSo, if we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x\\to a^+} f(x) = L,\n$$\n\nthen we may conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} f(x) = L.\n$$\n\nFor a multivariable function, the situation is not so straightforward. For a function of two variables $f(x,y),$ we may only conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f(x,y) = L,\n$$\n\nif we can show that the limit is the same *for all possible paths* on which $\\mathbf x$ can approach $\\mathbf{x}_0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4b46a0a-5b1c-58eb-aa32-dd0ccd57f7a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2482 :tutorial/title "The Definition of a Limit: Single Variable Functions vs. Multivariable Functions"} :step/id #uuid "a9b09430-2391-566d-ac41-6360e135665e" :step/math-academy-id 11094 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125473}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125303 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124267 :question/id #uuid "56539c63-dd81-57c9-b7d2-18c935953203" :question/math-academy-id "e-3366"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407879}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"} :step/id #uuid "b6bdeefa-e28b-5e72-8c05-e9f8fb9c7542" :step/math-academy-id 11197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125760 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we may need to simplify a limit before we can evaluate it. \n\nFor example, let's consider the limit\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2}.\n$$\n\nDirect substitution causes this limit to take the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 00$ because the numerator and denominator both evaluate to zero at the given point. \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = \\dfrac{(2)^2 + 2(2)(-1)}{(2)(-1) + 2(-1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by factoring the numerator and denominator and then canceling a common factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy}{xy+2{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{y(x+2y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = -2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8f5c0d1f-e38c-5cd7-97e2-1aaaac5158b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2883 :tutorial/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "6285e0db-558c-5e11-85ab-0b82dc834963" :step/math-academy-id 15456 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125296}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124564 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124373 :question/id #uuid "ae167d95-9cfd-5f31-91b1-484becd62677" :question/math-academy-id "e-6239"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050231}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407881}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "af7ce556-917b-54ac-a772-adecff1e36f2" :step/math-academy-id 23425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125473 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125815 :tutorial/content "However, it is often easy to show that a limit does not exist for a multivariable function. All we have to do is find two paths on which the limit evaluates to different results. \n\nAs an example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)} \\dfrac{y}{x+y}\n$$\n\nClearly, direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00.$ Let's compute the limit along the paths $y=x$ and $y=x^2.$ We will see that the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{y}{x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{x+x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{1+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{y}{x +y} = \\text{DNE}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8040690e-6d4c-50ac-8b9f-f92615717816" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3960 :tutorial/title "Proving That a Limit Does Not Exist by Evaluating It Along Different Paths"} :step/id #uuid "29991074-71d2-5a37-a971-5f658f37d3ba" :step/math-academy-id 23498 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125303}}] :activity/title "Limits and Continuity of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124730}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046665} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407884} :activity/id #uuid "a4f417dd-5f5b-58a7-ab74-5a973fc151bb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048455 :topic/id #uuid "a05758d1-f2b4-5a86-b7b0-c2a4bb6dcb85" :topic/math-academy-id 1931} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377714 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8958422f-4c02-53af-a3e5-28f8445d5bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377716} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7dc054-8a42-5213-b120-534bafd7ab86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377717} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad9cc3c-2948-54af-96ab-97ac3dc5dd75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377718} {:answer/id #uuid "de9768d4-de86-55ac-a72b-b0470b48216c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377719} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2466e9-2081-57a2-b9cc-0260610356d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377719} :answer-field/id #uuid "f14349ec-0b87-5b7c-b87c-2368f3340f77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6323a167-9fc1-5848-81f1-63d34a9c63ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-74965" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot 0}{{0}^{3}+{0}^{3}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths. \n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=2x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+8{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "23171e8d-210d-5c2c-9bfd-bb23fda5150e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1408b7a6-0c53-5b8e-9133-67f17a92be59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407856} {:answer/id #uuid "195220e5-a0bf-53a4-9715-d520b9cc06cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407858} {:answer/id #uuid "4272af7f-33bd-5340-9cab-dc535b5219ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407861} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9919cf-e851-589f-8631-eb084d75d2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407864} {:answer/id #uuid "af13b1b6-a010-5d4f-9f5a-9200139b2880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407861} :answer-field/id #uuid "356ed38c-7b44-5965-81ac-2c88dd16e0ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d70f2b2e-153e-52a8-b763-677074637b88" :question/math-academy-id "q-74955" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}-2}{{2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2}-2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2})^{2}-4}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy-2}{(xy-2)(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} & =\\frac{1}{(2\\cdot 1+2)(\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{16}.$"} :step/id #uuid "61b825c0-1272-5e4c-b3ff-4f0f75ec0886" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407882}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041632fd-d218-5815-9c69-da107f5da160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407854} {:answer/id #uuid "091e3fce-c81a-50bf-8d66-819204f1ee13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407855} {:answer/id #uuid "4d84526b-47f3-51fb-a168-2cba08c88949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407862} {:answer/id #uuid "e7aeae6b-4c5c-573f-8a2d-c1af8e88ebf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407872} {:answer/id #uuid "f5036257-dfe1-524a-b3ae-379280f91c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407872} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b798538-abe6-5e7d-a0f2-143f5a975750" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9a907de-0404-5ffb-8362-e9676298070b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36139" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n- The limit evaluates to $-1$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2}$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $\\frac{1}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-{x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2(-{x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "80843e02-eff6-534a-9c80-13ab1c07a9fb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194293e4-0f48-56b7-8094-db759002b936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407857} {:answer/id #uuid "638f4f88-2af6-5eb2-9ed5-e5ceca460c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407863} {:answer/id #uuid "88efc86f-081f-55bf-ae7a-919620a60363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407866} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3f8034-9dc3-50c6-bd5e-b2ea032262ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407867} {:answer/id #uuid "ada75cf0-6667-5096-ac74-83cbe5d47353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407868}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407866} :answer-field/id #uuid "926f9639-4822-59a3-b1ce-00790077ea5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "113d22b7-0222-5499-8d44-de1af29900a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-129340" :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & =\\frac{{0}^{3}-2(0)}{{2}^{3}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{y({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{\\cancel{y}({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}\\cancel{y}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "dd8e3137-e924-50eb-b2e3-83c1445d70ab" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407886}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f90210-da8d-511b-9d38-b3d158c315d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407859} {:answer/id #uuid "4005330f-5c8b-5294-9f96-5e9be2626147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407860} {:answer/id #uuid "88b04b8f-a6d9-5b86-ba06-a0eb6a004245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407865} {:answer/id #uuid "b72aa7bf-e6dc-52f1-8358-bfb499369454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407870} {:answer/id #uuid "d17e2afc-964e-5d9b-b663-5d2974b50bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407860} :answer-field/id #uuid "e093674e-caf5-5f8d-97ff-ca3172fa6889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fac05de1-cec1-5741-a5ec-beb8567ada24" :question/math-academy-id "q-129343" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & =\\frac{1+(-1)}{\\sqrt{2-1}-\\sqrt{2+(-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z})(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(x-y)-(x+z)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{-(y+z)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{(y+z)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{\\cancel{(y+z)}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z}) & =-(\\sqrt{2-1}+\\sqrt{2+(-1)}) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "e8e33d31-3246-51ca-a270-794ee881c78b"}] :activity/title "Limits and Continuity of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407853}]}
